---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Postscript vs AfterShip Email: Marketing SMS or Shipping Communication?';
const description = 'A focused comparison of Postscript and AfterShip Email for Shopify messaging, consent, triggers, pricing, and operating ownership.';
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Can AfterShip Email replace Postscript?', a: 'No. AfterShip Email is centered on shipping and delivery communication, while Postscript is centered on consented marketing SMS. Choose based on the message job and keep operational and promotional permissions separate.' },
  { q: 'Can Postscript replace AfterShip Email?', a: 'Not for carrier-aware shipping updates and delivery exceptions. Postscript can support promotional SMS, but it should not become the source of truth for shipment status.' },
  { q: 'Can a store use both?', a: 'Yes, if ownership, consent, suppression, and quiet windows are documented. Test that a delivery notification does not trigger a promotional SMS without the required marketing permission.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="py-12 md:py-20"><div class="container max-w-4xl">
    <a href="/compare" class="text-sm text-muted inline-block mb-6">← All comparisons</a>
    <header class="mb-10"><p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-primary mb-3">Two-product Shopify comparison</p><h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-6">{title}</h1><p class="text-xl text-muted">Postscript and AfterShip Email can both touch a customer after checkout, but their ownership models differ. This page compares only those two products.</p></header>
    <section class="rounded-xl border border-primary/20 p-6 mb-10"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">Short answer</h2><p>Choose AfterShip Email when the job is carrier-aware operational communication: shipment updates, delivery confirmation, exceptions, and post-delivery service touchpoints. Choose Postscript when the job is consented marketing SMS such as cart recovery, promotions, and subscriber conversations.</p><p class="mt-3">They are not interchangeable because the trigger, permission, and success metric differ. A store can use both, but must keep operational notifications separate from promotional messages and never treat a shipping notification phone number as marketing consent.</p></section>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Decision snapshot</h2><div class="overflow-x-auto mb-10"><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Postscript</th><th>AfterShip Email</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Primary job</td><td>Marketing SMS and conversational retention</td><td>Shipping and delivery communication</td></tr><tr><td>Trigger source</td><td>Cart, subscriber, campaign, keyword, or marketing segment</td><td>Carrier, fulfillment, tracking, delivery, or exception event</td></tr><tr><td>Permission focus</td><td>Marketing SMS consent and opt-out</td><td>Operational notification expectations and customer data</td></tr><tr><td>Success measure</td><td>Recovery, attributed revenue, replies, and list health</td><td>Delivery visibility, support deflection, and service satisfaction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Postscript: when it fits</h2><p>Postscript is the better fit when a merchant wants SMS to recover carts, announce launches, communicate offers, or support subscriber replies. The marketing team needs a clear consent record, frequency policy, quiet hours, opt-out handling, and a way to attribute revenue without counting every purchase as an SMS increment.</p><p class="mt-3">Pilot one cart or promotional journey with a consented cohort. Test opt-out, duplicate enrollment, purchase suppression, replies, failed sends, and the gap after an operational delivery message. Pricing should be modeled around subscribers, sends, SMS costs, support, and campaign volume rather than a single monthly headline.</p>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-10 mb-4">AfterShip Email: when it fits</h2><p>AfterShip Email is more appropriate when the customer needs reliable, branded communication about a shipment’s state. Delivery and exception messages should be grounded in carrier and fulfillment data, with content that explains what happened and what the customer can do next.</p><p class="mt-3">The key test is event integrity. Verify tracking updates, delayed or failed delivery branches, cancellation and refund handling, localization, sender identity, and the point at which a message becomes promotional. Model order volume, carrier coverage, templates, seats, and support terms before scaling.</p>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-10 mb-4">Controlled pilot</h2><div class="overflow-x-auto mb-10"><table><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Postscript</th><th>AfterShip Email</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Entry</td><td>Marketing consent, cart, keyword, or campaign segment</td><td>Fulfillment, tracking, delivery, or exception event</td></tr><tr><td>Exit</td><td>Purchase, reply, opt-out, frequency cap, or campaign end</td><td>Delivery resolution, cancellation, refund, or service handoff</td></tr><tr><td>Failure</td><td>Invalid consent, duplicate send, carrier-state collision, or undelivered SMS</td><td>Stale tracking, wrong address, exception loop, or missing carrier update</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Consent, message, response, and attributed outcome</td><td>Event timestamp, tracking state, rendered notice, and support impact</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Final verdict</h2><p>Postscript wins for consented marketing SMS and conversational retention. AfterShip Email wins for operational shipping communication tied to carrier events. Mature Shopify teams may use both, provided the trigger map, permission model, suppression rules, and reporting ownership are explicit.</p><p class="mt-6">Continue with the <a href="/blog">Shopify email guides</a>, <a href="/use-cases/post-purchase">post-purchase playbook</a>, and <a href="/alternatives">alternatives library</a>.</p>
    <section class="mt-12"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">FAQ</h2>{faqs.map(f=><details class="border-b border-border py-4"><summary class="font-semibold">{f.q}</summary><p class="mt-3 text-muted">{f.a}</p></details>)}</section>
  </div></article>
</BaseLayout>
